  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is Bunburying?",
      "a": "Algernon's word for inventing an imaginary friend, the invalid Bunbury, as an excuse to escape social duties and do as he likes. Jack does the same with his invented brother Ernest.",
      "asks": [
        "Who is Bunbury?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does Jack call himself Ernest?",
      "a": "He uses the name Ernest in London to enjoy himself, pretending Ernest is his wicked younger brother. The irony is that he turns out really to be named Ernest.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is the play called Earnest?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is the handbag scene funny?",
      "a": "Lady Bracknell treats being found in a handbag in a station cloakroom as a social crime, reacting with outrage rather than sympathy for an abandoned baby. Wilde mocks how class snobbery outweighs feeling.",
      "asks": [
        "A handbag scene meaning"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a comedy of manners?",
      "a": "A witty play that satirises the customs and pretensions of a fashionable class, often through courtship plots. Restoration comedies and Wilde's plays are classic examples.",
      "asks": [
        "Comedy of manners definition"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What happened to Oscar Wilde?",
      "a": "In 1895 he was convicted of gross indecency for relationships with men and sentenced to two years' hard labour. He left prison bankrupt and died in Paris in 1900. In 2017 UK law pardoned men convicted under such laws.",
      "asks": [
        "Why was Oscar Wilde imprisoned?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The Importance of Being Earnest opened at the St James's Theatre in London on 14 February 1895.",
    "Its subtitle is A Trivial Comedy for Serious People.",
    "Wilde was sentenced in May 1895 to two years' hard labour."
  ],
